THE POLARITY RELAXATION — NOT APPLIED, FOR JEREMY'S RULING (2026-09-06)

WHAT IT IS.  Two constructors of strong.Conversion's judgment
(`conv-unseal`, `conv-seal`) are freed from their fixed polarity, so that
a reveal may sit at a `↓ˢ` position and a conceal at a `↑ˢ` one.  Nothing
else in the judgment changes.

WHY.  TyPeelR's repaired contractum mints `unsealAtᶜ 0 s`: the ∀-face's
identity leaves at the slot the instantiation just OWNED become the
instantiation itself — `unseal 0` covariantly, `seal 0` contravariantly.
Under an `↑ˢ` (reveal) ∀-face those land exactly where the judgment
allows them and the case is a theorem TODAY
(strong.proof.Preserve.preserve-TyPeelR-↑).  Under a `↓ˢ` (conceal)
∀-face — a polymorphic ARGUMENT that crossed a Peel, reachable from
closed plain source (Examples §13a) — every inserted leaf lands at the
opposite polarity, and the resulting face is MIXED:

    unsealAtᶜ 0 (id (` 0) ↦ seal 1)  =  seal 0 ↦ seal 1

    the inserted `seal 0` is CONTRAVARIANT, so it wants flip p ≡ ↓ˢ, i.e.
    p = ↑ˢ; the face's own `seal 1` is COVARIANT, so it wants p = ↓ˢ.

MACHINE-CHECKED, on an isolated copy of the tree (no commits):

  * WITH the relaxation, `TyPeelRCase` — the FULL case, polarity
    quantified — DISCHARGES: the hunks below make
    `preserve-TyPeelR : TyPeelRCase` a theorem, by exactly the `↑ˢ`
    proof with `p` carried through.  The only new content is that
    `⊢unsealAtᶜ`/`⊢sealAtᶜ` acquire the two leaf cases that were
    previously impossible (a `seal` at a covariant position and an
    `unseal` at a contravariant one), each closed by the fact that an
    owner of `abstN n (abst ∷ Ψ)` is never slot n itself (`abstN-≢`).

COLLATERAL, also machine-checked:

  * strong.proof.Canonicity §5 COLLAPSES.  `typed→canon` becomes FALSE
    (`conv-seal ez : Δ ⊢ seal 0 ∶ ℕ ⇝ ` 0 ∙ ↑ˢ`, while
    `Canon ↑ˢ (seal 0)` is empty), and with it `canon-pol` and
    `pol-unique` — the same non-identity conversion now types at BOTH
    polarities, which is precisely what `pol-unique` denies.  That is
    the honest reading of "p becomes vacuous": the right follow-up is to
    DROP `Pol` from the judgment altogether and delete §5, not to keep a
    polarity index that constrains nothing.
  * strong.proof.PreserveObstruct §2 dies: `¬seal↦seal` is refuted by
    `conv-fun (conv-seal ez) (conv-seal (es ez)) ∙ ↓ˢ`, so
    `¬TyPeelRCase` goes and Examples §13a's facts (ii) and (iii) become
    the positive statement that the contractum TYPES.
  * strong.proof.IdPushReach.idPush⁺ needs two `{p = ↑ˢ}` annotations
    (the polarity is no longer forced by `conv-unseal`); included below.
    This is cosmetic — nothing about IdPush changes.
  * NOTHING ELSE.  strong.Progress, strong.proof.Canonical,
    strong.proof.IdLayer, strong.proof.Adversary, strong.proof.PeelDual,
    strong.proof.CancelFaces and strong.TermSubst check unchanged: no
    canonical-forms lemma and no progress case reads a polarity.

HOW TO APPLY (from the repo root):
  git apply --directory=SystemF/agda \
    SystemF/agda/strong/notes/polarity-relaxation.patch
(checked with `git apply --check`; it applies cleanly to this tree.)
Then delete `¬seal↦seal`/`¬⊢t-contractum`/`¬TyPeelRCase` (PreserveObstruct
§2), replace Examples §13a's `¬J-face`/`¬⊢J-mint` by the positive
typings, and either delete Canonicity §5 or drop `Pol` from the
judgment.  DO NOT apply without Jeremy's ruling: this is a change to the
conversion judgment, not to a rule.

